# Part 1: The Server Room Era (1990s-2000s)

## A Day in the Life of a Sysadmin

**6:00 AM** - Pager goes off. Server down.

**6:30 AM** - Drive to the data center (yes, physically)

**7:00 AM** - Badge in, find the rack, diagnose hardware failure

**9:00 AM** - Order replacement part. ETA: 3-5 business days.

**9:15 AM** - Set up redirect to backup server (manually)

**12:00 PM** - Meeting: "Why was the site down for 6 hours?"

---

# The Physical Reality

## What companies actually owned

**Your Company's Data Center:**

- Rack 1: $50k
- Rack 2: $50k
- Rack 3: $50k
- UPS Backup: $20k
- Cooling System: $30k
- Fire Suppression: $15k

**Staff Required:**

- Sysadmins (24/7 on-call)
- Network Engineers
- Security Team

**Total upfront cost:** $200k-500k+ before writing a line of code

---

# The Painful Truth

## Why the old way hurt

| Problem | Reality |
|---------|---------|
| **Capacity Planning** | Guess traffic 6 months ahead (usually wrong) |
| **Black Friday** | Hope you bought enough servers in June |
| **Server Idle Time** | 90% idle, but you still pay for 100% |
| **Hardware Failure** | One disk dies = site goes down |
| **Expansion** | 3-month procurement cycle |

**The conversation:**

> Developer: "We need more capacity"
>
> Finance: "Fill out this form. Budget review is Q3."
>
> Developer: "But the traffic spike is next week..."

---

# ANTI-PATTERN: Cowboy Deployment

## How people actually deployed code (please don't do this)

```bash
# The FTP era
$ ftp production-server.com
ftp> put index.php
ftp> quit

# The SSH-and-pray method
$ ssh admin@production-server.com
$ cd /var/www/html
$ vim index.php    # Edit directly in production!
$ sudo service apache restart
$ # ...pray it works
```

**What could go wrong?**

- No version control -> "What was the old code?"
- No rollback -> "Can we undo this?" "No."
- "It works on my machine!" -> Different PHP versions
- No testing -> Customers find your bugs

