# Part 3: First Principles - Why Docker

## The "Works on My Machine" Problem

**Your laptop:**

- Python 3.11
- PostgreSQL 15
- Ubuntu 22.04
- That weird library you installed 6 months ago

**Production server:**

- Python 3.9
- PostgreSQL 13
- CentOS 7
- None of your weird libraries

**Result:** Hours debugging environment differences

---

# ANTI-PATTERN: Global Dependencies

## The dependency disaster

```bash
# Installing dependencies globally (DON'T DO THIS)
$ sudo pip install flask
$ sudo pip install requests==2.25.0

# 6 months later, different project...
$ sudo pip install requests==2.28.0
# Breaks your first project!

# Even worse: system Python
$ sudo apt install python-something
# Overrides system utilities that depend on Python
```

**The problem:** Everything shares the same environment

**The solution:** Isolation

---

# Linux Primitives: The Building Blocks

## Containers aren't magic--they're Linux features

```mermaid +render
flowchart TB
    subgraph Primitives["Linux Building Blocks"]
        CG["cgroups<br/>(Resource Limits)"]
        NS["namespaces<br/>(Isolation)"]
        CH["chroot<br/>(Filesystem)"]
    end

    subgraph Result["Container"]
        PROC["A process that thinks<br/>it has its own computer"]
    end

    CG --> Result
    NS --> Result
    CH --> Result
```

**The insight:** A container is just a process with restrictions

---

# cgroups: The Resource Police

## "You can't use more than this"

**The problem:** One rogue process can:

- Eat 100% of RAM -> everything crashes
- Peg all CPU cores -> everything freezes
- Fill the disk -> system unbootable

**The solution:** cgroups (Control Groups)

```bash
# Limit a process to 512MB RAM and 50% CPU
# (This is what Docker does under the hood)
```

| What cgroups do | Example |
|-----------------|---------|
| **Limit** resources | Max 512MB RAM |
| **Track** usage | "Used 2GB this hour" |
| **Prioritize** | Web server gets more CPU than batch jobs |

---

# namespaces: The Isolation Magic

## "You can't see other processes"

**The problem:** By default, every process can see:

- All other processes (`ps aux`)
- The whole network (same IP, same ports)
- The entire filesystem

**The solution:** namespaces

| Namespace | What it isolates |
|-----------|------------------|
| **PID** | Process list (container sees PID 1) |
| **Network** | IP addresses, ports |
| **Mount** | Filesystem tree |
| **User** | User/group IDs |

**Try it yourself:**

```bash
# Create a process that can only see itself
sudo unshare --pid --fork --mount-proc ps aux
```

---

# chroot: The Filesystem Trick

## "Your root is not my root"

**The problem:** How do you give a process a different filesystem without a VM?

**The solution:** `chroot` (change root)

```bash
# Make /my-container the new "/" for this process
sudo chroot /my-container /bin/bash

# Now "/" points to /my-container
# Can't see the real filesystem!
```

**Fun fact:** This has existed since 1979!

**Used for:**

- Building Linux distributions from scratch
- Rescuing broken systems from a live USB
- Creating containers (combined with cgroups + namespaces)

---

# DEMO: chroot in Action

## Live demonstration

1. Create a minimal filesystem
1. chroot into it
1. Show isolated filesystem view
1. Exit and compare

---

# How Primitives Become Containers

```mermaid +render
flowchart TB
    subgraph Linux["Linux Kernel"]
        CG["cgroups<br/>Limit: 512MB RAM<br/>Limit: 50% CPU"]
        NS["namespaces<br/>Own PID 1<br/>Own network<br/>Own filesystem view"]
        CH["chroot<br/>Root = /var/lib/docker/..."]
    end

    subgraph Container["What you see"]
        PROC["nginx:alpine<br/>Thinks it owns the machine<br/>Actually just a process"]
    end

    CG --> Container
    NS --> Container
    CH --> Container
```

**The magic:** Docker packages this complexity into a simple CLI

---

# Why Docker Won

## It's all about developer experience

**Before Docker:** To use cgroups/namespaces, you needed to:

- Write C code
- Understand kernel interfaces
- Be a Linux expert

**After Docker:**

```bash
docker run nginx
```

That's it. One command.

---

# What Docker Provides

<!-- column_layout: [1, 1] -->

<!-- column: 0 -->

**1. Dockerfile**

Recipe for building images

```dockerfile
FROM node:18
WORKDIR /app
COPY . .
RUN npm install
CMD ["npm", "start"]
```

**Why it matters:** Reproducible environments

**2. Image Layers**

Cacheable, reusable pieces

- Each instruction = one layer
- Layers shared between images
- Only rebuild changed layers

**Why it matters:** Fast builds

<!-- column: 1 -->

**3. Docker Hub**

Central image registry

```bash
docker pull nginx
docker pull postgres
docker push mycompany/myapp
```

**Why it matters:** Share with the world, reuse others' work

**4. Simple CLI**

Anyone can use it

```bash
docker run nginx        # Start container
docker build -t app .   # Build image
docker push app         # Share image
```

**Why it matters:** Low barrier to entry

<!-- reset_layout -->

**The insight:** Docker didn't invent containers--it made them usable

---

# Container Runtimes & OCI

## What actually runs your containers?

<!-- column_layout: [1, 1] -->

<!-- column: 0 -->

**What is a Container Runtime?**

The software that actually runs containers:
- Starts/stops containers
- Manages resources (cgroups)
- Provides isolation (namespaces)
- Handles container lifecycle

**Popular Runtimes:**

**Docker Engine**
- Most popular, full platform
- Use: Development, most production

**containerd**
- Docker's underlying engine
- Use: Kubernetes, lightweight

**CRI-O**
- Built for Kubernetes
- Use: K8s-focused deployments

**Podman**
- Daemonless, Docker-compatible
- Use: Rootless containers, security

<!-- column: 1 -->

**OCI (Open Container Initiative)**

Industry standard for containers

**What it defines:**
- Container image format
- Runtime specification
- Distribution specification

**Why it matters:**

✓ **Compatibility**
- Docker image runs on Podman
- Write once, run anywhere

✓ **No vendor lock-in**
- Switch runtimes easily
- Not tied to Docker

✓ **Innovation**
- Any company can build tools
- Interoperable ecosystem

**Example:**
```bash
# Build with Docker
docker build -t myapp .

# Run with Podman (OCI-compliant)
podman run myapp  # Works!
```

**Fun fact:** Docker donated containerd to OCI, now used by many tools!

<!-- reset_layout -->

---

# Containers vs VMs

```mermaid +render
flowchart TB
    subgraph VM["Virtual Machine"]
        APP1["App"]
        OS1["Guest OS<br/>(Ubuntu)"]
        HV["Hypervisor"]
        OS0["Host OS"]
        HW1["Hardware"]

        APP1 --> OS1 --> HV --> OS0 --> HW1
    end

    subgraph Container["Container"]
        APP2["App"]
        RT["Container Runtime"]
        OS2["Host OS"]
        HW2["Hardware"]

        APP2 --> RT --> OS2 --> HW2
    end
```

| Aspect | VM | Container |
|--------|-----|-----------|
| **Startup** | 30s - 5min | < 1 second |
| **Size** | 1-50 GB | 10-500 MB |
| **Isolation** | Complete (own kernel) | Shared kernel |
| **Use case** | Different OS needed | Same OS, different apps |

---

# Hypervisors & VM Tools

## Understanding virtualization layers

<!-- column_layout: [1, 1] -->

<!-- column: 0 -->

**Type 1 Hypervisors (Bare Metal)**

Run directly on hardware (no host OS)

**Examples:**
- VMware ESXi
- KVM (Linux)
- Xen
- Microsoft Hyper-V

**Characteristics:**
- Better performance
- Production-ready
- Hardware-level control

**Use case:**
- Data centers
- Production servers
- Cloud infrastructure

<!-- column: 1 -->

**Type 2 Hypervisors (Hosted)**

Run on top of host OS

**Examples:**
- VirtualBox
- VMware Workstation
- QEMU
- Parallels (Mac)

**Characteristics:**
- Easier to set up
- Better for development
- Runs alongside host apps

**Use case:**
- Development
- Testing
- Learning

**VM Management Tools:**
- **libvirt** - KVM/QEMU management (CLI/API)
- **multipass** - Quick Ubuntu VMs
- **Vagrant** - Reproducible VM environments (VMs as code)

<!-- reset_layout -->

**When to use VMs vs Containers:**
- **VMs:** Different OS, complete isolation, legacy apps
- **Containers:** Same OS, faster startup, modern cloud-native apps

