# Dockerfile Syntax: Complete Reference

## All instructions you need

```dockerfile
# FROM: Base image (required, must be first)
FROM python:3.11-slim

# ARG: Build-time variables (can use in FROM)
ARG APP_VERSION=1.0

# LABEL: Metadata
LABEL maintainer="you@example.com" version="${APP_VERSION}"

# WORKDIR: Set working directory (creates if doesn't exist)
WORKDIR /app

# COPY: Copy files from host to container
COPY requirements.txt .

# RUN: Execute commands during build (creates layer)
RUN pip install -r requirements.txt

# COPY: Add application code
COPY . .

# ENV: Environment variables (available at runtime)
ENV PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1 PORT=8080

# USER: Switch to non-root user (security!)
RUN useradd -m appuser
USER appuser

# EXPOSE: Document port (metadata only, doesn't publish)
EXPOSE 8080

# VOLUME: Mount point for persistent data
VOLUME /data

# HEALTHCHECK: Container health check
HEALTHCHECK --interval=30s --timeout=3s \
  CMD curl -f http://localhost:8080/health || exit 1

# ENTRYPOINT: Main executable (harder to override)
# CMD: Default arguments (easy to override)
ENTRYPOINT ["python"]
CMD ["app.py"]
```

**Most-used instructions:** FROM, WORKDIR, COPY, RUN, ENV, EXPOSE, CMD

---

# CMD vs ENTRYPOINT vs RUN

## The confusion explained

<!-- column_layout: [1, 1] -->

<!-- column: 0 -->

| Instruction | When? | Override? |
|-------------|-------|-----------|
| **RUN** | Build time | No |
| **CMD** | Container start | Yes (easy) |
| **ENTRYPOINT** | Container start | Yes (harder) |

**Use cases:**

- **RUN:** Install packages, setup during build
- **CMD:** Default command (easy to change)
- **ENTRYPOINT:** Force executable (args only)

<!-- column: 1 -->

**Examples:**

```dockerfile
# 1. CMD only (fully overridable)
CMD ["python", "app.py"]
```
```bash
docker run myimage python other.py  # Works!
```

```dockerfile
# 2. ENTRYPOINT + CMD (args overridable)
ENTRYPOINT ["python"]
CMD ["app.py"]
```
```bash
docker run myimage other.py  # Runs: python other.py
```

```dockerfile
# 3. RUN (build time only)
RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y curl
```
No override - happens once during `docker build`

<!-- reset_layout -->

---

# Dockerfile Layer Caching

## Why order matters

```dockerfile
# BAD: Changes to code invalidate all layers
FROM python:3.11-slim
COPY . .                          # Changes often!
RUN pip install -r requirements.txt  # Reinstalls every time

# GOOD: Dependencies cached separately
FROM python:3.11-slim
COPY requirements.txt .           # Changes rarely
RUN pip install -r requirements.txt  # Cached unless requirements change
COPY . .                          # Changes often, but doesn't affect above
```

**Each instruction = new layer**

**Layers are cached and reused**

**Change a layer = rebuild that layer + all after it**

**Pro tip:** Order from least-changed to most-changed

