# Part 4: Git Branching & CI/CD

## Why Branching Matters

**Without branches:**

```bash
# Developer A
git commit -m "Add login feature"
git push  # Pushes broken code

# Developer B (same time)
git commit -m "Fix homepage"
git push  # Conflict! Merge mess!

# Production: broken
```

**With branches:**

```bash
# Developer A
git checkout -b feature/login
# Work safely in isolation

# Developer B
git checkout -b fix/homepage
# Work safely in isolation

# Merge when ready, reviewed, tested
```

---

# GitHub Flow (Keep It Simple)

```mermaid +render
gitGraph
    commit id: "main"
    branch feature
    commit id: "work"
    commit id: "more work"
    checkout main
    merge feature id: "PR merged"
    commit id: "deployed"
```

**The rules:**

1. `main` is always deployable
1. Branch for every change (no matter how small)
1. Open a Pull Request for review
1. Merge triggers deploy

**Why this works:** Small changes = small risk

---

# How Branches Enable CI/CD

## Different branches, different actions

**The insight:** Branch-based automation

**Push to feature branch:**

- Run tests
- Run linters
- Build (to verify it works)
- Block merge if fails

**Merge to main:**

- Run all tests
- Build production artifacts
- Deploy to staging (optional)
- Deploy to production
- Tag release

**The flow:**

```
feature/new-login → [Tests only]
         ↓
    (Pull Request)
         ↓
      main → [Tests + Build + Deploy] → Production
```

---

# Part 5: CI/CD

## What is CI? (Continuous Integration)

**The problem:** "Integration hell"

- Work on a feature for 2 weeks
- Try to merge
- 500 conflicts
- 2 more weeks fixing conflicts

**The solution:** Merge early, merge often, test automatically

**The CI pipeline:**

1. `git push` → Triggers automation
2. Build → Compile/bundle the code
3. Test → Run all tests
4. Lint → Check code style
5. **Pass** → Ready to merge
6. **Fail** → Fix required (can't merge)

---

# ANTI-PATTERN: Long-Lived Branches

## The merge disaster

```mermaid +render
gitGraph
    commit id: "Jan"
    branch feature
    commit id: "Feb work"
    checkout main
    commit id: "Feb fixes"
    checkout feature
    commit id: "Mar work"
    checkout main
    commit id: "Mar changes"
    commit id: "Apr changes"
    checkout feature
    commit id: "Apr work"
    checkout main
    merge feature id: "May: NIGHTMARE"
```

**3-month-old branch:** 200 files changed, 50 conflicts

**vs. daily merges:** 5 files changed, 0-2 conflicts

---

# What is CD? (Continuous Deployment)

**The old way:**

1. Finish feature
1. Schedule deploy meeting
1. Write deploy checklist (20 steps)
1. Execute checklist manually at 2am Sunday
1. Something breaks
1. Rollback manually
1. Postmortem Monday

**The CD way:**

1. Merge to main
1. Tests pass automatically
1. Deploy happens automatically
1. Done

---

# GitHub Actions: Free CI/CD

## Why GitHub Actions won

- **Free** for public repos (2,000 min/month for private)
- **Integrated** with GitHub (status checks, PR comments)
- **Huge marketplace** of pre-built actions
- **Matrix builds** (test on multiple OS/versions)
- **Self-hosted runners** (use your own machines)

```yaml
# .github/workflows/ci.yml
name: CI

on: [push, pull_request]

jobs:
  test:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: actions/setup-python@v5
        with:
          python-version: '3.11'
      - run: pip install -r requirements.txt
      - run: pytest
```

---

# GitHub Actions: Complete Workflow Syntax

## Full example with all key concepts

```yaml
# .github/workflows/ci.yml
name: CI/CD Pipeline

# Triggers - when to run
on:
  push:
    branches: [main, develop]
    paths: ['src/**']           # Only if these files change
  pull_request:
    branches: [main]
  schedule:
    - cron: '0 0 * * 0'         # Weekly on Sunday
  workflow_dispatch:            # Manual trigger (button in UI)

# Global environment variables
env:
  NODE_ENV: production

# Jobs (run in parallel by default)
jobs:
  test:
    name: Run Tests
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest      # or: windows-latest, macos-latest

    # Matrix: test multiple versions
    strategy:
      matrix:
        python-version: ['3.9', '3.10', '3.11']
        os: [ubuntu-latest, macos-latest]
      fail-fast: false          # Continue even if one fails
    # Creates 6 jobs: 3 versions × 2 OS = 6

    # Services: databases, cache, etc.
    services:
      postgres:
        image: postgres:15
        env: {POSTGRES_PASSWORD: postgres}
        ports: [5432:5432]

    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4  # Checkout code (almost always first)

      - uses: actions/setup-python@v5  # Use pre-built action
        with:
          python-version: ${{ matrix.python-version }}
          cache: 'pip'

      - run: |                       # Run shell commands
          pip install -r requirements.txt
          pytest --cov

      - name: Upload coverage        # Upload artifacts
        uses: actions/upload-artifact@v3
        with:
          name: coverage-report
          path: htmlcov/

  build:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    needs: test                      # Job dependency (waits for test)
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - run: docker build -t myapp:latest .

  deploy:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    needs: [test, build]             # Wait for both jobs
    if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'  # Conditional: only on main
    environment:
      name: production
      url: https://myapp.com
    steps:
      - run: echo "Deploying..."
```

**Key concepts:** Triggers, jobs (parallel/sequential), matrix builds, services, secrets

---

# GitHub Actions: Secrets & Variables

## Managing sensitive data

**Secrets (encrypted, never logged):**

```yaml
steps:
  - name: Deploy
    env:
      API_KEY: ${{ secrets.API_KEY }}
      DB_PASSWORD: ${{ secrets.DB_PASSWORD }}
    run: ./deploy.sh
```

**Add secrets in:** Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions

**Variables (not encrypted, visible in logs):**

```yaml
env:
  API_URL: ${{ vars.API_URL }}
  ENVIRONMENT: ${{ vars.ENVIRONMENT }}
```

**Built-in context variables:**

```yaml
- name: Print info
  run: |
    echo "Branch: ${{ github.ref_name }}"
    echo "Actor: ${{ github.actor }}"
    echo "Repo: ${{ github.repository }}"
    echo "SHA: ${{ github.sha }}"
```

---

# GitHub Actions: Common Patterns

## Real-world examples

**Test → Build → Deploy:**

```yaml
name: Production Pipeline

on:
  push:
    branches: [main]

jobs:
  test:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - run: npm test

  build:
    needs: test
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - run: docker build -t app .
      - run: docker push app:${{ github.sha }}

  deploy:
    needs: build
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - run: kubectl set image deployment/app app=app:${{ github.sha }}
```

**Pull Request checks:**

```yaml
name: PR Checks

on:
  pull_request:

jobs:
  lint:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - run: npm run lint

  type-check:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - run: npm run type-check

  test:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - run: npm test
```

