# Part 6: The Cloud

## What is "The Cloud"

**Not helpful:** "It's just someone else's computer"

**Actually helpful:** Abstraction over infrastructure

**The layers:**

**What You See:**

- APIs & Dashboards
- Simple commands (`create vm`, `upload file`)

**What Cloud Provides (Abstraction):**

- **Compute** - Run your code anywhere
- **Storage** - Save data reliably
- **Network** - Connect everything together

**What Actually Exists (Reality):**

- Data centers globally distributed
- Thousands of physical servers
- Networking equipment
- Power, cooling, security
- Teams managing hardware

**The value:** You never touch the hardware, just use the API

---

# Why Cloud Won (For Most Use Cases)

| Old Way | Cloud Way |
|---------|-----------|
| Buy servers ($50k) | Pay $0.10/hour |
| Wait 3 months | Ready in 5 minutes |
| Guess capacity | Scale automatically |
| Hire sysadmins | Managed services |
| One data center | 30+ global regions |
| You fix outages | SLA guarantees |

**The killer insight:** Convert capital expense to operating expense

No more begging finance for server budget

---

# ANTI-PATTERN: Over-Engineering for Scale

## You don't need Kubernetes (yet)

**Startup with 100 users:**

> "We need microservices with service mesh,
> Kubernetes, and multi-region failover!"

**Reality:** A $5/month VPS would work fine

**When you actually need scale:**

- Millions of users
- Sub-second latency requirements globally
- Strict uptime SLAs
- Team of 50+ engineers

Start simple. Add complexity when you need it.

---

# Scaling: Vertical vs Horizontal

## Two ways to handle more load

<!-- column_layout: [1, 1] -->

<!-- column: 0 -->

**Vertical Scaling (Scale Up)**

Add more resources to ONE server

**Example:**
- t2.micro → t2.large → t2.xlarge
- 2GB RAM → 8GB RAM → 32GB RAM

**Pros:**
- ✓ Simple setup
- ✓ No code changes
- ✓ No distributed system complexity

**Cons:**
- ✗ Hardware limits (biggest machine)
- ✗ Single point of failure
- ✗ Expensive at scale
- ✗ Downtime to upgrade

**When to use:**
- Starting out
- Databases (simpler)
- Legacy applications

<!-- column: 1 -->

**Horizontal Scaling (Scale Out)**

Add MORE servers

**Example:**
- 1 server → 3 servers → 10 servers
- Distribute load across fleet

**Pros:**
- ✓ Almost unlimited scaling
- ✓ Redundancy (if one fails, others continue)
- ✓ Cheaper (commodity hardware)
- ✓ No downtime (add/remove servers)

**Cons:**
- ✗ Requires stateless architecture
- ✗ More complex (load balancers, orchestration)
- ✗ Eventual consistency challenges

**When to use:**
- Production web apps
- High traffic services
- Cloud-native apps
- Modern microservices

**Cloud makes this easy:** Click to add servers, pay per use

<!-- reset_layout -->

---

# Basic Cloud Services

## The core categories

<!-- column_layout: [1, 1] -->

<!-- column: 0 -->

**Compute (Run Code)**

**VMs (Virtual Machines)**
- Full control, run anything
- Examples: EC2, Compute Engine, Azure VMs
- When: Legacy apps, full OS control

**Containers (Managed)**
- Run Docker containers
- Examples: Cloud Run, ECS, Fargate
- When: Microservices, scaling

**Functions (Serverless)**
- Just upload code
- Examples: Lambda, Cloud Functions
- When: Event-driven, sporadic loads

**Network**

- **Load Balancers** - Distribute traffic
- **CDN** - Cache globally (fast!)
- **DNS** - Route traffic

<!-- column: 1 -->

**Storage (Save Data)**

**Object Storage**
- Files, images, videos, backups
- Examples: S3, Cloud Storage, Azure Blob
- Cheap, unlimited scale
- Pay per GB stored

**Databases - Managed**

*Relational (SQL):*
- PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQL Server
- ACID guarantees
- Complex queries

*NoSQL:*
- MongoDB (documents)
- DynamoDB (key-value)
- Firestore (real-time)
- Flexible schema
- Horizontal scaling

**Why managed?** Cloud provider handles:
- Backups
- Updates
- Scaling
- High availability

